-- The runtime adapter's contracts (LUA-5): readiness through a supervised wait,
-- owner identity, finite submission and both its bounds, exactly-once settlement,
-- host failures under the owner's policy, protected stop, independent owners and
-- the refused reply wait from inside a Lua callback, plus the adapter's
-- dependency closure. Every execution and lifecycle example acquires real VMs
-- under a real `withSupervision`; the structural checks need no VM. The
-- scheduling machinery's examples (LUA-21) are here too, under the
-- `Test.LuaRuntime.Scheduler` modules: they run scripted segments under an
-- injected clock and a fake timer, fire every wake by hand and need no VM. The
-- scheduled owners' examples (LUA-6) are under `Test.LuaRuntime.Scheduled`: they
-- run real Lua tasks as coroutines on real supervised owners with the same
-- injected clock and fake timer, and coordinate with gates and STM signals. It is
-- a suite of its own, in its own source directory, beside `lua-host-tests`, which
-- keeps the host's examples unchanged.
test-suite lua-runtime-tests
